-- D6 state foundation, tier D: state supreme courts, the courts of last
-- resort in the 38 states (40 courts, since Texas and Oklahoma each split
-- their highest court into civil and criminal branches) where at least
-- some form of popular vote -- contested election or retention -- governs
-- who sits on the bench. Judiciary is the most structurally varied tier
-- built in this project: selection method differs not just by state but
-- often by court level within a state, so this migration is deliberately
-- scoped to SUPREME COURTS ONLY -- intermediate appellate and trial
-- courts (thousands of judges nationwide, no comparable per-state
-- summary source) are explicitly out of scope for this pass.
--
-- Owner decision (2026-08-12, asked explicitly before building anything):
-- retention elections -- 17 of the 40 courts here, where a sitting
-- justice faces a plain yes/no "keep or remove" vote with no opposing
-- candidate -- are modeled as is_elected=TRUE. Voters genuinely have a
-- ballot role and a real accountability lever even without a contested
-- race; the owner judged this fits the app's core mission (did this
-- official keep faith with voters) at least as well as an ordinary race.
--
-- Classification source and a real contradiction it resolved: Ballotpedia
-- publishes two different summary tables that look like they should
-- agree and don't -- a general "judicial election methods by state" table
-- (which mixes ALL court levels together) said New York holds partisan-
-- primary/nonpartisan-general elections for its judges, while the
-- court-specific "State supreme courts" comparison chart said New York's
-- Court of Appeals is governor-appointed through a nominating commission.
-- Resolved via general knowledge, not guessed: New York's confusingly-
-- named "Supreme Court" is actually a TRIAL-level court whose justices
-- ARE elected -- the state's actual court of last resort is the
-- separately-named Court of Appeals, which is genuinely appointed. This
-- is why every classification in this migration comes from the
-- court-specific comparison chart's "Method of selection for full-term"
-- column, not the general table, and why New York has no rows here at
-- all (excluded, pure appointment, no retention).
--
-- States entirely excluded (10, no rows): Connecticut, Delaware, Hawaii,
-- Maine, Massachusetts, New Hampshire, New Jersey, New York, Rhode Island
-- -- pure gubernatorial appointment (with or without legislative
-- confirmation), no subsequent voter role of any kind, ever; and South
-- Carolina, Virginia -- elected BY THE LEGISLATURE, not by voters, same
-- exclusion precedent already used throughout this project for
-- legislature-selected executive offices (Maine's Attorney General,
-- etc.).
--
-- Real per-court structural variation, captured via each court's own
-- title text rather than a single hardcoded assumption: most states pool
-- all justices under one identical title (modeled as one at_large office,
-- seat_count = N, same multi-seat pattern used for PSC/legislature
-- multi-member districts); Alabama and Texas separately elect a Chief
-- Justice as its own distinct seat (own office row, not folded into the
-- associate-justice pool); Mississippi and Texas seat their justices by
-- numbered District/Place, each a genuinely distinct single-seat office.
--
-- how_obtained per justice is NOT uniformly 'elected' -- each state's own
-- page includes an "Appointed by" column distinguishing a justice who won
-- their current seat outright ("Elected") from one currently serving an
-- INTERIM vacancy appointment from a named governor, not yet having faced
-- any voter test. The latter get how_obtained='appointed' even in a
-- fully elected/retention state, same precision this project has applied
-- elsewhere (e.g. a governor who succeeded via resignation vs. one who
-- won election outright).
--
-- Real vacancies preserved, not filled: e.g. Mississippi shows 8 of 9
-- constitutional seats (District 3 Position 2 vacant in the source),
-- same "real gap, not guessed away" discipline used throughout this
-- project.
--
-- Term length is real per-state data (6 to 12 years, from the same
-- "State supreme courts" comparison chart), NOT a uniform default the way
-- several earlier migrations in this project used -- e.g. Alabama 6yr,
-- Louisiana/Illinois/Pennsylvania/Wisconsin 10yr, California/Missouri
-- 12yr. Oklahoma's chart entry read "Initial term 1 year; if retained,
-- 6 years" -- 6 used here, the regular full term.



